<Base
  lang="en"
  alternativa="/politica-de-privacidade/"
  sobreposto={false}
  titulo="Privacy Policy | Bertoncini & Mendonça Advogados"
  descricao="Privacy Policy of Bertoncini & Mendonça Advogados: what personal data we process, why, who we share it with, how long we keep it and how to exercise your rights."
>
  <article class="politica">
    <div class="env">
      <p class="chapeu">Legal document</p>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="atualizado">Version 1.0, effective from November 18, 2026</p>

      <nav class="sumario" aria-label="Sections of this policy">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ol>
          {secoes.map((secao) => (
            <li><a href={`#${secao.id}`}>{secao.rotulo}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="texto">
        <h2 id="quem-somos">Who we are and what this Policy is for</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>BERTONCINI & MENDONÇA ADVOGADOS</strong>, a law firm registered with
          the Brazilian Bar Association (OAB), Paraná Section, under No. 24,788,
          enrolled in the CNPJ under No. 69.076.965/0001-64, headquartered in the city
          of Maringá, Paraná (the “Firm” or “we”), provides judicial and
          out-of-court legal services in civil and contract law, digital law, estate
          and succession planning, real estate law and corporate law.
        </p>
        <p>
          This Policy explains, in a straightforward way, what personal data we
          process, why we process it, who we share it with, how long we keep it and how
          you can exercise your rights. It applies to clients and prospective clients;
          to family members, heirs, business partners and other people whose data
          reaches us in the course of a matter; to opposing parties and third parties
          involved in the matters we advise on; to suppliers, partners and applicants;
          and to anyone who visits our website or our social media, or who talks to us
          by WhatsApp and e-mail (the “Data Subject” or “you”).
        </p>
        <p>
          The use of cookies and similar technologies is detailed in our{' '}
          <a href="/en/cookie-policy/">Cookie Policy</a>, which forms part of this
          document.
        </p>
        <p>
          In most situations, the Firm acts as controller, because it decides how and
          why data is processed in order to provide the services engaged. When a
          corporate client gives us data on its employees or on third parties for a
          purpose it defines, such as an internal audit, we may act as processor,
          following that client’s instructions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Everything you entrust to us in the course of a matter is protected by the
          attorney confidentiality provided for in the Statute of the Legal Profession
          (Law No. 8,906/1994) and in the OAB Code of Ethics and Discipline. This
          protection is in addition to the safeguards of the General Personal Data
          Protection Law.
        </p>

        <h2 id="definicoes">Definitions</h2>
        <p>For ease of reading, these terms have the following meaning in this Policy:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>LGPD</strong>: Law No. 13,709/2018, Brazil’s General Personal Data Protection Law.</li>
          <li><strong>ANPD</strong>: the National Data Protection Authority, the body that oversees compliance with the LGPD.</li>
          <li><strong>Personal data</strong>: information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person, such as name, CPF, e-mail or IP address.</li>
          <li><strong>Sensitive personal data</strong>: data on racial or ethnic origin, religious belief, political opinion, membership of a trade union or of a religious, philosophical or political organization, health, sex life, genetic or biometric data.</li>
          <li><strong>Processing</strong>: any operation carried out with personal data, such as collection, use, storage, sharing and deletion.</li>
          <li><strong>Controller</strong>: whoever makes the decisions about the processing. Processor: whoever processes data on behalf of the controller.</li>
          <li><strong>Data Protection Officer</strong>: the person appointed by the Firm to act as the communication channel with data subjects and with the ANPD.</li>
          <li><strong>Cookies</strong>: small files stored in the browser when you visit a website, explained in the <a href="/en/cookie-policy/">Cookie Policy</a>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="dados">What data we process, for what purpose and on what legal basis</h2>
        <p>
          We process only the data needed for each purpose, and each processing
          operation relies on one of the legal bases of arts. 7 and 11 of the LGPD. As
          a rule, the data comes from you; it may also come from whoever engaged us,
          from public sources (property registrations, commercial registries, court
          records, notary and registry offices) or from automated tools on the website.
        </p>
        <div class="tabela tabela-larga">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Situation</th>
                <th scope="col">Data processed</th>
                <th scope="col">Purposes</th>
                <th scope="col">Legal basis (LGPD)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {tratamentos.map((linha) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{linha.situacao}</th>
                  <td data-rotulo="Data processed">{linha.dados}</td>
                  <td data-rotulo="Purposes">{linha.finalidades}</td>
                  <td data-rotulo="Legal basis (LGPD)">{linha.base}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          We do not make decisions based solely on automated processing that affect
          your interests. All legal work is conducted and reviewed by the Firm’s
          lawyers.
        </p>

        <h2 id="familiares">Data of family members, children and adolescents, and sensitive data</h2>
        <p>
          Estate and succession planning often involves the whole family. To structure
          a gift, a will, a family holding company or a probate proceeding, we may need
          data on children, grandchildren, stepchildren, spouses and former spouses,
          including children and adolescents, even if these people are not our
          clients.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Children and adolescents.</strong> This data is always processed in
          the best interest of the child or adolescent (art. 14 of the LGPD) and
          limited to the minimum necessary, such as identifying an heir or donee.
          Processing may rely on the legal bases of arts. 7 and 11 of the LGPD, in
          accordance with CD/ANPD Statement No. 1/2023. Where the basis is consent, it
          will be given by at least one parent or by the legal guardian. Our website is
          not directed at people under 18, who should not send us data through it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sensitive data.</strong> Some services require sensitive data, such
          as health information for guardianship, supported decision-making or advance
          healthcare directives, or religious beliefs that must be respected in
          testamentary provisions. We process this data only when indispensable, with
          access restricted to the lawyers responsible for the case, on the basis of
          the regular exercise of rights in contracts and in judicial, administrative
          or arbitration proceedings (art. 11, II, “d”), compliance with a legal
          obligation (art. 11, II, “a”) or, where no other basis applies, your specific
          and highlighted consent (art. 11, I).
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not ask for sensitive data through the website. For this reason, we ask
          that you do not describe health matters or other sensitive information in the
          contact form; these details can be dealt with in a meeting, already under
          attorney confidentiality.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Data of other people that you send us.</strong> When you share data
          of family members or third parties, you declare that you are entitled to do
          so for the purpose of the matter. In these situations, the attorney
          confidentiality guaranteed by the Statute of the Legal Profession may limit
          what we tell these third parties about the service engaged, always within
          what the law allows.
        </p>

        <h2 id="compartilhamento">Who we share your data with</h2>
        <p>
          We do not sell personal data. We share only what is necessary for each
          purpose, always preserving attorney confidentiality, with:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Notary and registry offices, commercial registries, public bodies and the Judiciary, when the service requires it, such as when executing deeds, registering corporate amendments or paying taxes.</li>
          <li>Professionals who support the case, such as accountants, property appraisers, experts, translators, correspondent lawyers and partner law firms, all bound by confidentiality.</li>
          <li>Technology suppliers acting as processors, including Hostinger (institutional e-mail), Microsoft (document editing and storage), Vercel (website hosting), Brevo (sending contact form messages and the newsletter) and the management, electronic signature and accounting systems. Any artificial intelligence tools used are engaged under a confidentiality obligation, without the Firm’s data being used to train their models, and every output is reviewed by a lawyer.</li>
          <li>Google, Meta and LinkedIn, only with respect to the browsing data collected by the cookies you authorize, in accordance with the <a href="/en/cookie-policy/">Cookie Policy</a>.</li>
          <li>Authorities, upon a legal request or court order, and third parties, when necessary for the regular exercise of the Firm’s rights.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We do not send lists of clients or contacts to advertising platforms, for
          example to build custom audiences, and we do not nominate clients to legal
          publications or rankings without prior and specific authorization.
        </p>

        <h2 id="transferencia">International transfer</h2>
        <p>
          Some tools we use store or process data outside Brazil, mainly in the United
          States. This is the case with Microsoft’s OneDrive, Hostinger’s e-mail, Vercel, which hosts the website, and Brevo, which sends contact form messages and the newsletter,
          which may involve processing on servers abroad, and with the services of
          Google (Analytics, Ads and YouTube), Meta (Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp)
          and LinkedIn.
        </p>
        <p>
          These transfers follow art. 33 of the LGPD and take place through standard
          contractual clauses approved by CD/ANPD Resolution No. 19/2024 or another
          valid mechanism. For non-essential cookies, the transfer also relies on the
          consent you give in the banner, having been informed in advance of the
          international nature of the operation (art. 33, VIII). If you would like to
          know which suppliers and countries are involved, write to our Data
          Protection Officer.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prazo">How long we keep your data</h2>
        <p>
          We keep data for as long as it is needed for the purpose and, after that,
          for the period required by law or necessary for the defense of rights. The
          reference periods are:
        </p>
        <div class="tabela">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Data</th>
                <th scope="col">Retention period</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {prazos.map((linha) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{linha.dados}</th>
                  <td data-rotulo="Retention period">{linha.prazo}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Cookies</th>
                <td data-rotulo="Retention period">As set out in the table in the <a href="/en/cookie-policy/#cookies-utilizados">Cookie Policy</a></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Once the period ends, the data is deleted or anonymized, except in the cases
          of retention under art. 16 of the LGPD, such as compliance with a legal
          obligation and the regular exercise of rights.
        </p>

        <h2 id="seguranca">Security and incidents</h2>
        <p>
          We adopt technical and administrative measures appropriate to the size of
          the Firm and to the nature of the data we process: two-factor authentication
          on all accounts, encryption of data in transit and of data stored on
          OneDrive, access to each case restricted to those working on it, devices
          with up-to-date systems and encrypted disks, as well as a duty of
          confidentiality for everyone with access to the Firm’s information and
          contracts with data protection clauses with our suppliers.
        </p>
        <p>
          No system is completely immune to failure. If a security incident occurs
          that may result in relevant risk or harm, we will notify the ANPD and the
          affected data subjects within the period set by CD/ANPD Resolution No.
          15/2024, currently 3 business days, explaining what happened and the
          measures taken.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you suspect any misuse of your data involving the Firm, write immediately
          to <a href={`mailto:${DPO}`}>{DPO}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="direitos">Your rights and how to exercise them</h2>
        <p>
          You may request, at any time, by e-mail to{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${DPO}`}>{DPO}</a>:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>confirmation that we process your data and access to it;</li>
          <li>correction of incomplete, inaccurate or outdated data;</li>
          <li>anonymization, blocking or deletion of unnecessary or excessive data, or of data processed in breach of the LGPD;</li>
          <li>data portability, under the ANPD’s regulations;</li>
          <li>deletion of data processed on the basis of your consent and revocation of that consent;</li>
          <li>information about who we share your data with and about the possibility of not consenting and its consequences;</li>
          <li>objection to processing that breaches the LGPD and review of decisions made solely on the basis of automated processing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We may ask for information to confirm your identity before responding, so
          that your data is not handed over to someone else. Confirmation of
          processing and access in simplified form are provided immediately; the full
          statement, within 15 days (art. 19 of the LGPD).
        </p>
        <p>
          Some requests may be met only in part where there is a duty of attorney
          confidentiality, a legal obligation to retain data, a need to defend rights
          in proceedings, or data of other people involved. In these cases, we will
          explain why. You may also file a complaint with the ANPD.
        </p>

        <h2 id="whatsapp">WhatsApp, communications and scams</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>WhatsApp.</strong> We use WhatsApp Business for scheduling and
          day-to-day matters. WhatsApp is a Meta service, which processes conversation
          data as a controller, in accordance with its own privacy policy. For
          documents containing health information, bank details or asset details, we
          prefer institutional e-mail or a secure OneDrive sharing link.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Communications.</strong> You can stop receiving our articles and
          invitations at any time, through the unsubscribe link in every message or by
          writing to the Data Protection Officer’s e-mail. Messages about the progress
          of your case continue to be sent for as long as there is a contract.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Beware of scams.</strong> Criminals have been using the names and
          photos of lawyers, and even real case data, to ask for payments. For this
          reason, keep these rules in mind:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>We never ask for payment to release funds, court release orders, compensation or inheritances.</li>
          <li>Fees are charged only by boleto or PIX in the name of BERTONCINI & MENDONÇA ADVOGADOS, CNPJ 69.076.965/0001-64.</li>
          <li>Our e-mails are sent exclusively from the @bertoncinimendonca.adv.br domain, and our official telephone number is <a href="tel:+5544988157540">+55 (44) 98815-7540</a>.</li>
          <li>If in doubt, call one of the official numbers before clicking on links, sending data or paying.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="encarregado">Data Protection Officer, changes and applicable law</h2>
        <p>
          Data Protection Officer: Juscelino Bertoncini. Deputy Data Protection
          Officer: Beatriz Mendonça. E-mail: <a href={`mailto:${DPO}`}>{DPO}</a>.
          Mailing address: Rua Marechal Deodoro, 530, apartamento 142, Zona 7,
          Maringá/PR, CEP 87.030-020, Brazil.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Changes.</strong> This Policy may be updated to reflect changes in
          the law, in our services or in the tools we use. The version and effective
          date are always shown at the beginning of the document, and relevant changes
          will be highlighted on the website and, where appropriate, communicated by
          e-mail.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Governing law and jurisdiction.</strong> This Policy is governed by
          Brazilian law, in particular the LGPD, the Brazilian Civil Rights Framework
          for the Internet (Law No. 12,965/2014) and the Statute of the Legal
          Profession (Law No. 8,906/1994). The courts of the Judicial District of
          Maringá, Paraná, are chosen as the forum, without prejudice to the data
          subject’s right to bring proceedings in the courts of their domicile where
          the law so guarantees and the right to petition the ANPD.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
